>&#x0002b;</sup> T cells from spleens of 3-d reinfected mice cultured in vitro with <italic>C. albicans</italic> antigen and accessory macrophages.  &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TF3-154"><label>&#x000b6;</label><p>&#x02009;P &#x0003c;0.05 Student's <italic>t</italic> test (rIL-4&#x02013;treated versus untreated mice).  &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TF3-160"><label>&#x0002a;&#x0002a;</label><p>&#x02009;P &#x0003c;0.05 Student's <italic>t</italic> test (anti&#x02013;IL-4 mAb-treated versus untreated IL-4<sup>&#x0002b;&#x0002f;&#x0002b;</sup> mice).              &#x000a0;</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Effect of IL-4 on cytokine production (<italic>A</italic>) and IL-4R expression (<italic>B</italic>) on neutrophils  and macrophages from IL-4<sup>&#x02212;/&#x02212;</sup>  (<italic>solid columns</italic>) or IL-4<sup>&#x0002b;/&#x0002b;</sup> (<italic>dotted  columns</italic>) mice. Elicited peritoneal  neutrophils or macrophages were  cultured in vitro with IFN-&#x003b3; &#x0002b;  LPS or <italic>C. albicans</italic> cells. Priming  with IL-4 was done by adding  250 U/ml of rIL-4 8 h before <italic>C.  albicans</italic> (<italic>striped columns</italic>, <italic>A</italic>; and  lane <italic>2</italic>, <italic>B</italic>). After 24 h incubation,  supernatants were assayed for cytokine contents and cells for IL-4R expression by RT-PCR. *,  Cytokine levels below the detection limit of the assay, as indicated on the y-axis. Lane <italic>1</italic>, <italic>C.  albicans</italic> alone. <italic>N</italic>, No DNA  added to the amplification mix  during PCR; <italic>C</italic>, HPRT- or cytokine-specific controls.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JEM971224.f6a"/><graphic xlink:href="JEM971224.f6b"/></fig></sec><ack><p>The authors are grateful to Eileen Mahoney Zannetti for dedicated secretarial and editorial assistance.</p><p>This study was supported by AIDS Project 50A.0.28, Italy. The Basel Institute of I